/// Datasheet for the `rigid` device — a 2D RIGID-BODY engine with ROTATION over oriented boxes (OBBs),
/// the full-fat companion to the branch-free AABB `physics` device. Separate device, distinct component
/// names, so a driver may use both side by side.
///
/// A rigid body (`RBody`, defined in rigid.arche) is a centre `pos` (the shared `vec` 2-vector) plus:
///   `lvel` = linear velocity (x, y)
///   `spin` = (ANGULAR velocity, —)            — a 1-scalar packed in a 2-vector's x (see note)
///   `rot`  = (cos θ, sin θ)                    — orientation as a UNIT VECTOR (no trig intrinsic exists)
///   `ext`  = (half-width, half-height)         — the box is centre ± rotate(ext)
///   `mi`   = (inverse mass, inverse inertia)   — both 0 ⇒ an immovable static body
///   `mat`  = (restitution, friction)           — material response coefficients
///   `dimp` = linear-impulse accumulator (x, y)         — the Jacobi solver's velocity accumulators…
///   `dang` = (angular-impulse accumulator, —)          — …committed by `apply`
///   `cor`  = (active-contact count, —)                 — per-body, from the `contacts` pass; the divisor
///                                                        for mass-splitting (the primary Jacobi stabiliser)
/// EVERY per-body scalar is packed into the x of a 2-vector ON PURPOSE: a scalar-float column read through
/// a self-join (`All.scalar`) mis-types as int, and 3-VECTOR components mis-handle in kernels — only plain
/// 2-vectors read/write cleanly both directly and through the boids reduce. The driver owns and sizes the
/// pool; `[4]RBody` is a mild storage floor (the device works with any count).
/// A 3-vector used only as a `func` return/local (normal.xy + penetration from `sat`); it never sits on a
/// body — 3-vector KERNEL columns mis-handle, but 3-vectors are fine inside funcs.
pos3(x, y, z) :: float;

lvel(x, y) :: float;
spin(x, y) :: float;
rot(x, y)  :: float;
ext(x, y)  :: float;
mi(x, y)   :: float;
mat(x, y)  :: float;
dimp(x, y) :: float;
dang(x, y) :: float;
cor(x, y)  :: float;

/// `sup` = the SUMMED CONTACT NORMAL on a body: Σ (normal A→B) over every body B it is currently touching,
/// written by the optional `supports` pass. It answers "what am I touching, and in what DIRECTION?" — which
/// the contact COUNT in `cor.x` cannot. World +y is down, so a body resting ON something has `sup.y > 0`
/// (the normal points from it down into its support), and a body pressed against a wall to its RIGHT has
/// `sup.x > 0`. A character controller needs exactly this: `sup.y > 0` is a real GROUNDED test (a wall
/// contact alone must not let you jump), and the sign of `sup.x` says which way a wall is, so you can stop
/// driving into it (driving into a wall generates a large normal impulse, and friction against that impulse
/// dwarfs gravity — that is what makes a character STICK to walls). `supports` is optional and cheap enough
/// to run once per FRAME rather than per substep.
sup(x, y)  :: float;

[4]RBody;
